 <h2 id="overview">Purpose and learning objectives</h2>
 <p>Landing a jet on a carrier is what the Navy's carrier qualification instruction calls the thing that sets a Navy-trained jet pilot apart, and the most demanding phase of training: weeks of preparation for a few moments at the ship. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('1')}</a> Ships carry far more than carrier jets, though. Marine Corps helicopters, tiltrotors and jump jets fly from amphibious assault ships, and Army aviation has flown missions from Navy and Coast Guard ships in nearly every major operation since the Second World War, from medical evacuation and resupply to attack helicopter operations. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('1-1')}</a></p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to name the parts of a carrier's flight deck and what the arresting gear and catapult do; read the flight deck by jersey colour; describe a catapult launch; say which recovery case the weather calls for; fly the Case I pattern position by position; read the ball and explain why corrections shrink in close; answer the <Term id="Landing Signal Officer (LSO)">LSO</Term>'s calls and read a grade; fly a <Term>bolter</Term>, a <Term>waveoff</Term> and a <Term>Delta</Term>; explain tanking and <Term>bingo</Term>; describe a helicopter's <Term id="Charlie pattern">Charlie pattern</Term> and the <Term id="Landing Signal Enlisted (LSE)">LSE</Term>'s role; work out Army deck landing currency; and explain how a jump jet operates from an amphibious ship.</p>
 <p class="note">This is a reference, not a NATOPS manual or a syllabus. Carrier numbers are the <Term record="t-45">T-45</Term> training jet's, from its 2001 carrier qualification instruction; the ship and air traffic procedures come from a Navy controller training manual, and the Army procedures from a 1997 field manual. Current ship, air wing and service manuals govern and may differ. The instrument recovery has its own module, <a href={caseIII}>Case III Recoveries</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="ships">The ships and who controls the air</h2>
 <p>Three kinds of ship carry aircraft in these sources. <strong>Aircraft carriers</strong> (CV and CVN) launch and recover fixed-wing jets with catapults and arresting gear; a Nimitz-class carrier has four of each and can launch as many as four aircraft a minute. <a href="#source-fas-cvn">[5]</a> <strong>Amphibious assault ships</strong> (LHA and LHD) carry a Marine landing force, its helicopters and its jump jets, with a well deck in the stern for landing craft; the Tarawa-class LHA's full-length flight deck can handle ten helicopters at once as well as the <Term record="av-8b">AV-8 Harrier</Term>, and the Wasp-class LHD that followed it carries two more helicopters and three air-cushion landing craft. <a href="#source-fas-amphib">[6]</a> <strong>Single- and dual-spot ships</strong>, such as frigates with one landing spot aft, take helicopters only, and the Army calls landing on them the most demanding deck landing training. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('1-11, 1-13')}</a></p>
 <p>Around each ship sit two pieces of airspace. The <strong>control area</strong> is a circle of 50 miles radius from the surface up, run by the carrier's <Term id="CATCC">CATCC</Term> or the amphibious ship's <Term id="AATCC">AATCC</Term>; the <strong>control zone</strong> is the inner 5 miles up to 2,500 ft, the responsibility of the Air Officer in visual conditions. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-2–11-3')}</a> The Air Officer, the <Term id="Air Boss">Air Boss</Term> to aircrew, works from <Term id="Primary Flight Control (PriFly)">PriFly</Term> high in the island, watches the deck and the pattern and sets the case of departure and recovery from the weather. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('65')}</a> <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-7, 11-21')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="deck">The carrier flight deck</h2>
 <p>Ashore the same jets need runways of 5,000 to 8,000 ft; on a carrier they stop within 350 ft of touching down. <a href="#source-abe">{abe('3-1')}</a> The deck does it with two machines at opposite ends, and with a landing area angled away from the bow so that a jet that misses the wires can fly off again without hitting the aircraft parked forward.</p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="deck" aria-labelledby="deck-title">
  <h3 id="deck-title" class="fig-title">Figure 1 — A Nimitz-class flight deck (plan view)</h3>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 1, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 300" role="img" aria-label="Plan view of a Nimitz-class flight deck with its angled landing area, four wires, four catapults, jet blast deflectors, elevators, island, lens and LSO platform marked"></svg></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Select a part to highlight it. Traced to scale from an overhead photograph of the deck of Nimitz: the outline, island, elevators, catapults, wires and angled deck follow the photograph, with two catapults in the bow, two on the angled deck, two elevators forward of the island on the starboard side and one on either side of the landing area aft. Jet blast deflector, lens and LSO platform positions are read from the same photograph and are approximate; individual ships of the class differ. <a href="#source-navalgazing">[11]</a> <a href="#source-wiki-nimitz">[10]</a> <a href="#source-fas-cvn">[5]</a></p>
  <ol class="callouts">
   <li data-part="angle"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Angled landing area</button><p>The landing area is angled about 10° to port of the ship's axis, but the pattern is flown parallel to the <Term id="Base Recovery Course (BRC)">base recovery course</Term>, the ship's heading. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('31')}</a> The landing course, the <Term id="Final Bearing">final bearing</Term>, is the extended centerline of the angled deck, so the wind straight down the ship, <Term id="Axial winds">axial wind</Term>, crosses the landing area from right to left. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-4')}</a> <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('65')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-part="wires"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Arresting wires</button><p>The tailhook catches a <Term id="Cross-Deck Pendant">cross-deck pendant</Term>, a steel wire rope 1 7/16 in thick with a breaking strength of at least 205,000 lb. <a href="#source-abe">{abe('3-31')}</a> It pulls purchase cables off an arresting engine below deck, driving a ram into a cylinder of fluid metered out through a valve until the aircraft stops; the engine is set for each aircraft's weight before the recovery. <a href="#source-abe">{abe('3-1, 3-3')}</a> A Nimitz-class carrier has four wires; the last two of the class, Ronald Reagan and George H. W. Bush, have three, because the fourth was rarely used. <a href="#source-fas-cvn">[5]</a> <a href="#source-wiki-nimitz">[10]</a></p></li>
   <li data-part="foul"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Foul lines</button><p>Painted on both sides of the landing area, the <Term id="Foul line">foul lines</Term> mark the space that must be clear before the deck is clear. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('66')}</a> Before a recovery the waist catapult's gear is stowed, the <Term id="Jet blast deflector (JBD)">jet blast deflectors</Term> are lowered, the wires are reset and the arresting gear officer's switch lights the green clear-deck light. <a href="#source-abe">{abe('3-1, 3-3')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-part="ramp"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Ramp</button><p>The <Term>ramp</Term> is the aft end of the deck, curved down at the round-down. <Term id="Hook to ramp">Hook to ramp</Term> is the clearance between the hook point and the deck as the jet crosses it, which the lens protects by keeping the pilot on the glideslope. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('67–68')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-part="lens"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Lens</button><p>The optical landing system stands on the port side abeam the landing area and shows the pilot the <Term>ball</Term>. Its roll angle is set for each aircraft type to keep the hook's touchdown point the same whatever the distance from the pilot's eye to the hook. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('3, 68')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-part="lso"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">LSO platform</button><p>The <Term id="Landing Signal Officer (LSO)">landing signal officers</Term> stand on the port side aft. In the T-45 pattern the pilot starts the approach turn abeam the LSO platform. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('33')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-part="cats"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Catapults</button><p>A Nimitz-class carrier has four steam catapults. <a href="#source-fas-cvn">[5]</a> The waist catapults lie in the landing area, so their shuttles are moved aft and covered before a recovery. <a href="#source-abe">{abe('3-1')}</a> Steam from the ship's boilers is stored in an accumulator and released through a launch valve into the cylinders; the C-13 family's power stroke is 250 to 310 ft long. <a href="#source-abe">{abe('4-1')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-part="jbd"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Jet blast deflectors</button><p>Behind each catapult a <Term id="Jet blast deflector (JBD)">jet blast deflector</Term> rises from the deck: water-cooled panels that shield people, equipment and other aircraft from the exhaust of the jet at full power on the catapult. <a href="#source-abe">{abe('5-1')}</a></p></li>
   <li data-part="island"><button type="button" aria-pressed="false">Island and PriFly</button><p>The island holds the bridge and, seven stories above the deck, Primary Flight Control. <a href="#source-fas-cvn">[5]</a> The <Term id="Air Boss">Air Boss</Term> there runs the deck and every aircraft within 10 miles. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('65')}</a></p></li>
  </ol>
 </section>

 <h2 id="jerseys">Who wears which jersey</h2>
 <p>On a deck this busy nobody has time to ask. Every crew member's job shows in the colour of the jersey and helmet, and only the yellow shirts may move an aircraft. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('35')}</a> The Navy and the Coast Guard use the same colours, and the Army's shipboard manual teaches them to Army crews. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('F-1')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Flight deck jersey colours" tabindex="0"><table data-jerseys>
  <caption>Jersey colours on the flight deck <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('35')}</a> <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('F-1')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Jersey</th><th scope="col">Who</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr data-colour="Yellow"><th scope="row">Yellow</th><td data-who>Aircraft directors, the catapult and arresting gear officers, the flight deck officer and the helicopter LSE: the only people who may direct an aircraft</td></tr>
   <tr data-colour="Green"><th scope="row">Green</th><td data-who>Catapult and arresting gear crews, and squadron maintenance crews</td></tr>
   <tr data-colour="Brown"><th scope="row">Brown</th><td data-who>Plane captains, who look after an aircraft and watch its engine start</td></tr>
   <tr data-colour="Blue"><th scope="row">Blue</th><td data-who>Plane handlers who push, chock and chain aircraft, tractor drivers, elevator operators and phone talkers</td></tr>
   <tr data-colour="Purple"><th scope="row">Purple</th><td data-who>Fuel crews</td></tr>
   <tr data-colour="White"><th scope="row">White</th><td data-who>Safety observers, medical crews, LSOs, final checkers and quality assurance</td></tr>
   <tr data-colour="Red"><th scope="row">Red</th><td data-who>Ordnance crews, and crash and salvage crews</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Officers and chiefs on the flight deck also wear khaki trousers, and the catapult and arresting gear officers carry orange and green reflective tape on their helmets. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('35')}</a> A director's signals above the waist are for the pilot and those below it for the deck crew; a director hands an aircraft on by patting both sides of the head and pointing to the next director, who raises one arm to take control. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('36')}</a> If you lose sight of your director, or two give you signals at once, stop. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('35')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="jerseys" aria-labelledby="jerseys-title">
  <h3 id="jerseys-title">Quiz: what colour is that jersey?</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next</button></div>
 </section>

 <h2 id="launch">The catapult launch</h2>
 <p>In the T-45, the launch runs like this. The pilot taxis to the catapult behind yellow shirts, finishes the takeoff checklist and confirms the aircraft's gross weight on the weight board, rounded up to the next 500 lb, before crossing the <Term id="Jet blast deflector (JBD)">jet blast deflector</Term>; a weight more than 1,000 lb wrong goes to the tower by radio. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('35, 40')}</a> Then: <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('41–43')}</a></p>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Launch bar down.</strong> On the director's signal the <Term id="Launch bar">launch bar</Term> on the nose gear is extended and the jet taxis forward at a crawl until the bar drops over the <Term>shuttle</Term> and the <Term>holdback</Term> engages the catapult's buffer.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Tension</Term>.</strong> On the tension signal the pilot goes to full military power, releases the brakes with heels on the deck, raises the launch bar switch, grips the catapult handgrip and wipes out the controls. The jet squats as the shuttle takes up the slack.</li>
  <li><strong>Salute.</strong> Control passes to the catapult officer. When ready, the pilot salutes, head against the headrest. The catapult officer looks fore and aft and touches the deck; about a second later the jet accelerates to flying speed in about two seconds.</li>
  <li><strong>Off the bow.</strong> The T-45 must pass the bow at no less than 120 KIAS, or its minimum end speed if greater, then rotates to 10–12° nose up and climbs.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>Suspend</Term>.</strong> If anything is wrong before the launch, the pilot calls “suspend, suspend, suspend” and shakes the head, and keeps full power until the catapult officer walks in front of the wing and gives the throttle-back signal. In tension, hands stay below the canopy rails so that no gesture looks like a salute.</li>
 </ol>
 <p>On the deck side, the <Term id="Launch bar">launch bar</Term> rides a guide track that steers it into the shuttle's spreader automatically, so fewer people have to stand near the nose gear during hookup. <a href="#source-abe">{abe('5-15')}</a> Out of the catapult, a Case I jet stays at 500 ft parallel to the ship's course to 7 NM before climbing; in Case II it arcs at 10 NM to its departure radial if it cannot climb visually, and in Case III it climbs straight ahead at 300 kt through 5 NM at 1,500 ft or above before arcing. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-30')}</a> <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('43–44')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="cases">Case I, II and III</h2>
 <p>Weather decides how a ship launches and recovers its aircraft. The Air Officer picks the case; Case III is used at night, from half an hour after sunset to half an hour before sunrise, whatever the weather. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-21–11-22')}</a> Amphibious ships give helicopters lower limits: <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-9, 11-22, 11-57')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Recovery cases" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Ceiling and visibility at the ship <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-9, 11-22, 11-57')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Case</th><th scope="col">Flights will…</th><th scope="col">Carrier, and amphibious fixed wing</th><th scope="col">Amphibious ship helicopters</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term id="Case I, II and III">Case I</Term></th><td>not meet instrument conditions</td><td>3,000 ft and 5 NM or better</td><td>1,000 ft and 3 NM or better</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Case II</th><td>possibly meet instrument conditions</td><td>1,000 ft and 5 NM or better</td><td>500 ft and 1 NM or better</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Case III</th><td>meet instrument conditions</td><td>below 1,000 ft or below 5 NM</td><td>below 500 ft or below 1 NM</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>A Case I recovery holds overhead the ship and enters the pattern at the break. A Case II recovery is a controlled descent through cloud to the break and the visual pattern, never by more than a section of two in the T-45 instruction. A Case III recovery is an instrument approach all the way to the <Term>ball</Term>. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('29, 65')}</a> The cases also limit each other: after a Case III launch only a Case III recovery may follow, and during a Case I recovery the ship launches only Case I or II. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-22')}</a> The <a href={caseIII}>Case III Recoveries</a> module covers the marshal stack, the approach and its controllers in depth.</p>

 <h2 id="case1">The Case I recovery</h2>
 <p>Inbound in visual weather, the T-45 flight leader checks in with Marshal: call sign, number in flight, position, altitude, <Term>low state</Term> and lineup. Marshal gives the case, a holding altitude, the ship's weather and altimeter, the <Term id="Base Recovery Course (BRC)">BRC</Term>, <Term>bingo</Term> information and an expected approach time, and asks for a “see me”; with the ship in sight the leader calls “see you at ten”, or at the distance shown. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('28')}</a> The flight holds overhead in a counterclockwise circle no more than 5 NM across, tangent to the BRC with the ship at its 3 o'clock, no lower than 1,500 ft and separated from other flights by at least 1,000 ft. It descends only while abeam and aft of the ship, and on a “<Term>Charlie</Term>” it leaves the stack and heads for the initial. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('29')}</a> <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-7')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="pattern" aria-labelledby="pattern-title">
  <h3 id="pattern-title" class="fig-title">Figure 2 — The Case I pattern and the helicopter Charlie pattern</h3>
  <fieldset class="inline"><legend>Pattern</legend>
   <label><input type="radio" name="pattern" value="case1" checked /> Carrier Case I (T-45)</label>
   <label><input type="radio" name="pattern" value="charlie" /> Helicopter Charlie pattern (amphibious ship)</label>
  </fieldset>
  <div class="buttons chips" data-chips></div>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-play>Play</button></div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 2, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 380" role="img" aria-label="The landing pattern seen from astern and above, as a smooth ribbon with drop lines to the water showing the height at each position"></svg></div>
  <ol class="steps" data-steps="case1">
   <li data-step="initial"><strong>Initial.</strong> Arrive 3 NM astern, wings level at 800 ft and 250–300 KIAS, parallel to the BRC and just outboard of the starboard side, and call “flight of (number), initial”. Exterior lights go off before the break. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('30')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="break"><strong>Break.</strong> Over or past the bow, on the interval or no earlier than 1 NM past it, and never more than 4 NM ahead, the leader breaks left at 70–80° of bank and each wingman follows 15 seconds later. Level the break, descend to 600 ft once downwind, and lower gear and flaps below 200 KIAS. If the pattern is full, the tower says “<Term>spin</Term>”: a climbing left turn at the bow to 1,200 ft and another try. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('30–31')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="downwind"><strong>Downwind.</strong> At 600 ft, slow to on-speed angle of attack, check airspeed and complete the landing checklist. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('32')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="abeam"><strong>Abeam, which is the 180.</strong> At 0.9–1.1 NM abeam, the abeam and the 180 are the same point at the ship: turn abeam the LSO platform at 27–30° of bank with a 200–300 ft/min descent. With 25–30 kt of wind over the deck the ship is moving away from you. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('33')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="ninety"><strong>The 90.</strong> At 450 ft, increase the descent to 500 ft/min and keep 27–30° of bank. The ship looks high and tight because it is moving away; resist easing the turn and descending, which gives a low, overshooting start. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('31, 33')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="fortyfive"><strong>The 45.</strong> At 325–375 ft, start to pick up the ball and adjust the bank to roll out on the centerline. The LSO's “keep your turn in” here warns of an overshoot. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('33')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="groove"><strong>The <Term>groove</Term>.</strong> Roll wings level on the angled deck's centerline and call the ball: side number, aircraft type, “ball”, fuel <Term>state</Term>. Ideally 15–18 seconds long. No ball, call “<Term>Clara</Term>”, and never descend below 300 ft without one. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('18, 33')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="touchdown"><strong>Touchdown.</strong> Fly the ball all the way to the deck, on the centerline and on speed, and go to full power as the wheels touch. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('18, 34')}</a></li>
  </ol>
  <ol class="steps" data-steps="charlie" hidden>
   <li data-step="delta"><strong>Starboard <Term>Delta</Term>.</strong> Helicopters waiting for a deck hold in the starboard Delta, a right-hand racetrack about a mile to starboard at 300 ft, oriented on the BRC; during mixed operations with jets they must use it, and break at no more than 300 ft. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-2, 5-15')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="upwind"><strong>Upwind.</strong> Cleared by PriFly with a <Term>Charlie</Term>, the BRC, altimeter, wind and landing spot, the helicopter enters the <Term id="Charlie pattern">Charlie pattern</Term>, a left-hand racetrack on the port side whose upwind leg parallels the BRC; intervals are set upwind so the downwind leg is not stretched. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-15, 5-17')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="downwind"><strong>Downwind.</strong> Back down the port side toward the stern. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-15')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="turn"><strong>The turn in.</strong> The approach starts no later than abeam the landing spot; the turn meets the 45° line to the spot at the 90 position. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-18')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="fortyfive"><strong>The 45° line.</strong> The <Term id="Landing Signal Enlisted (LSE)">LSE</Term> picks up the helicopter at the 45 position in the turn. Fly straight in along the 45° line; loss of sight of the LSE on final means a wave-off. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-18–5-19')}</a></li>
   <li data-step="spot"><strong>The spot.</strong> Hover over the spot and land on the LSE's signals. To land in front of an occupied spot, stop the 45° approach abeam your spot and slide sideways into the hover, so the rotors keep clear. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-18')}</a></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. The numbered steps above give each position of the pattern.</p>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">Seen from astern and a little above, so that height reads straight up the page: the flight path is a smooth ribbon, its track is drawn on the water, and a drop line at each position shows its height from the T-45 carrier qualification instruction. Heights are exaggerated and the ship is drawn larger than scale. The Charlie pattern is shown as a track only, since the Army manual gives no pattern heights. The ship moves ahead during the pattern; the figure holds it still.</p>
 </section>
 <p>Case II starts the same check-in but holds at a fix on the ship's aft BRC at a distance of the holding altitude in thousands of feet plus 15. Cleared in, the T-45 descends at 250 KIAS and 4,000–6,000 ft/min to <Term>platform</Term> at 5,000 ft, slows its descent, levels at about 1,200 ft at 10 NM and arrives at 800 ft for the break and the normal pattern. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('29–30')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="lens">The lens and the ball</h2>
 <p>The Fresnel lens (<Term id="Fresnel lens optical landing system (FLOLS)">FLOLS</Term>) is a box 1 ft wide and 4 ft high of five light cells, the top four amber and the bottom one red, with a row of green <Term id="Datum lights">datum lights</Term> each side, four green <Term id="Cut lights">cut lights</Term> above and red <Term id="Wave-off lights">waveoff lights</Term> on each side. Each cell projects a bar of light that looks like a ball, the <Term>meatball</Term>; level with the datum lights you are on the glideslope, above them high, below them low, and a red ball is dangerously low. Never accept a low ball. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('2–3')}</a> The red waveoff lights, worked by the LSO, mean wave off now; the cut lights clear a pilot to land when radios are silent and call for power when lit again, and cut and waveoff lights flashing alternately mean go to your divert field. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('3, 23')}</a></p>
 <p>The cells together cover 1.5°, a wedge of light that the ball can be seen in from ¾° above to ¾° below the glideslope and about 20° either side of the centerline. Because it is a wedge, it narrows as you close: the training instruction's figure draws it 180 ft thick at a mile and only 15 ft thick at the <Term>ramp</Term>, so a correction that would barely move the ball at the start sends it off the lens in close. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('3, 5')}</a> The improved lens (IFLOLS) has twelve cells over 1.7° and can be read out to 1.5 NM; the same error looks bigger on it, so beware of overcontrolling. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('6, 8')}</a> The glideslope at the T-45's practice field is 3.25°. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('18')}</a></p>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="wedge" aria-labelledby="wedge-title">
