 </section>
 <p>Correcting takes three moves, says the instruction: for a high ball, less power to start down and the nose adjusted to hold on-speed angle of attack; as the ball centres, power back on to stop it; then a third adjustment to hold it. Low is the dangerous one: add power, and do not take it off again until the ball is centred. In close, stop a rising ball rather than chase it down; a big power reduction at the ramp, a “cut” or “eased gun”, gives a hard landing and is never acceptable. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('20–22')}</a> At the ship, a low ball needs a bigger power addition than at the field because of the wind over the deck, and the centerline keeps moving right as the ship sails, so scan lineup all the way to touchdown. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('31, 33')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="lso">LSO calls and grades</h2>
 <p>The <Term id="Landing Signal Officer (LSO)">LSO</Term>, call sign <Term>Paddles</Term>, is responsible for recovering fixed-wing aircraft safely aboard and for training pilots to land there, and grades and debriefs every pass. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('9')}</a> The calls are short, standard and come in three kinds: <strong>informative</strong> calls say what is happening, <strong>advisory</strong> calls warn of what is about to, and <strong>imperative</strong> calls order an action and must be obeyed at once. Being slow to answer a call at the ship can end a pilot's qualification. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('9–11, 34')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="LSO calls" tabindex="0"><table data-calls>
  <caption>Some LSO calls and the response <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('9–11')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Call</th><th scope="col">Kind</th><th scope="col">Response</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“You're a little high.”</th><td>Informative</td><td data-action>Adjust the rate of descent with power and nose attitude to recentre the ball.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“You're drifting left.”</th><td>Informative</td><td data-action>Stop the drift and correct back to the centerline.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“You're fast.”</th><td>Informative</td><td data-action>Adjust nose attitude and power to get back to optimum angle of attack.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Deep 90.”</th><td>Informative</td><td data-action>Compensate by increasing the pattern altitude.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Keep your turn in.”</th><td>Advisory</td><td data-action>Increase the bank to prevent an overshoot.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Don't settle.”</th><td>Advisory</td><td data-action>Adjust power and attitude so as not to settle below the glideslope.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Easy with the power.”</th><td>Advisory</td><td data-action>Make the power and nose correction smaller.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Power.”</th><td>Imperative</td><td data-action>Add power.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Right for lineup.”</th><td>Imperative</td><td data-action>Correct to the centerline, then level the wings.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Bolter.”</th><td>Imperative</td><td data-action>Full power, speed brakes in, rotate to optimum angle of attack and climb.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Wave off.”</th><td>Imperative</td><td data-action>Full power, speed brakes in, hold landing attitude and climb up the landing area centerline.</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row" data-call>“Drop your hook.”</th><td>Imperative</td><td data-action>Extend the arresting hook.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="calls" aria-labelledby="calls-title">
  <h3 id="calls-title">Quiz: answer the LSO</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next call</button></div>
 </section>
 <p>Each pass gets a grade on a 0 to 5 scale, recorded with comments in the pilot's record. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('11')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="LSO grades" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>LSO grades in T-45 carrier qualification <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('11')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Grade</th><th scope="col">Points</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">OK, underlined</th><td>5</td><td>A perfect pass</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">OK</th><td>4</td><td>Reasonable deviations with good corrections</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">(OK)</th><td>3</td><td>A fair pass, reasonable deviations</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">B</th><td>2.5</td><td>Bolter</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">— (no grade)</th><td>2</td><td>Below average but safe</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">PWO</th><td>2</td><td>Pattern waveoff</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">WO</th><td>1</td><td>Waveoff: not set up for a safe approach</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">C</th><td>0</td><td>Cut pass: unsafe, gross deviations inside the waveoff window</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">WOFD, TWO</th><td>No count</td><td>Waveoff for a foul deck; a practice waveoff</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>The comments are a shorthand: each fault has a symbol and each part of the groove a position, X at the start, IM in the middle, IC in close and AR at the ramp, with brackets meaning “a little” and underlining meaning “a lot”. So “LOIC” is low in close and “(F)X” a little fast at the start. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('12–13')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="outcomes">Arrestment, bolter, waveoff and Delta</h2>
 <p><strong>Arrestment.</strong> Fly the approach exactly as for a touch-and-go, ball to touchdown, and go to full power as the wheels touch: never anticipate the wire. Hold full power until the jet stops and the yellow shirt at about the 1 to 2 o'clock signals power back, then brakes off for the <Term id="Pull back">pull back</Term>, which lets the wire slip off the hook, and hook up. Braking during the pull back can tip the jet onto its tail. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('34')}</a> Then follow the yellow shirts clear of the landing area, past the <Term id="Foul line">foul line</Term>, with no more than 70% power. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('35')}</a></p>
 <p><strong><Term>Bolter</Term>.</strong> A touchdown in which the hook catches no wire. Because the pilot went to full power on touchdown, the jet is already flying: speed brakes in, rotate to optimum angle of attack, climb, and abeam the bow make a small turn right to parallel the BRC without crossing the bow, climb to 600 ft and turn downwind with the interval. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('34, 65')}</a></p>
 <p><strong><Term>Waveoff</Term>.</strong> Mandatory, by voice or by the red lights. Full power, speed brakes in, hold landing attitude, level the wings and climb straight up the angled deck, then turn parallel to the BRC abeam the bow. A <Term>waveoff</Term> may come for a <Term id="Foul deck">foul deck</Term>, winds out of limits or an approach that is not safe. A student may only take a waveoff unasked when the ball call has not been answered by the middle of the groove, and never in close. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('18–19, 34')}</a></p>
 <p><strong><Term>Delta</Term>.</strong> When the deck cannot take aircraft, after a foul deck or a run of bolters, the ship delays the recovery. PriFly signals it in Case I; in the pattern the T-45 holds at pattern altitude in the landing configuration at 130 KIAS with speed brakes in (“Delta Easy”), and the first aircraft to reach the 180 when cleared resumes the approach. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-43')}</a> <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('34, 66')}</a> In Case II and III, CATCC gives a Delta with the delay in minutes, always even and never less than four: aircraft in marshal keep holding, those that have started down above 7,000 ft level at the next lower odd altitude and hold at their altitude in thousands plus 15 miles, and those at or below 7,000 ft continue. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-43')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="fuel">Tanking and bingo</h2>
 <p>Fuel matters most when the ship is the only runway within reach. A recovery tanker, normally the one just launched, orbits the ship in a left-hand circle within 5 NM at no less than 1,500 ft by day and 2,500 ft at night in visual weather, flashing a green light while its refueling package is <Term>sweet</Term>. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-43–11-46')}</a> Departure control keeps track of its <Term>give</Term> and the low-state aircraft. When a jet is short of fuel the controller can tell the tanker to <Term>hawk</Term> it, positioning to be at its 2 o'clock if it bolters or waves off, or pass “<Term id="Trick or Treat">trick or treat</Term>” with the fuel it will need. Tanking is flown as a racetrack 3 to 5 miles abeam the ship and finished by 6 miles astern, which puts the receiver back in position for the approach. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-44–11-48')}</a></p>
 <p><strong><Term>Bingo</Term></strong> at the ship is not a fuel warning but an order: you are at emergency fuel and must fly to the shore field now. Marshal gives the <Term>pigeons</Term>, the bearing and distance to the divert field, and the bingo fuel at check-in, and updates them; write them down and check the ship's figures against your own chart. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('45')}</a> When bingo comes, turn and climb at once, clean, at full power on the planned profile, tell the ship but do not wait for an answer, squawk 7700, and descend at idle from the planned point. The T-45's profile arrives overhead with 300–500 lb, enough for one more circuit. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('45–47')}</a> Below a set fuel state, <Term id="Hold-down">hold-down</Term>, a jet is refueled on deck before it may launch again. <a href="#source-p1211">{cq('45')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="amphib">Amphibious assault ships</h2>
 <p>On an LHA or LHD, the <Term id="AATCC">AATCC</Term> controls aircraft in the 50-mile control area, <Term id="Primary Flight Control (PriFly)">PriFly</Term> controls the 5-mile control zone visually, and an embarked tactical air control squadron directs missions over the beach. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-49')}</a> Arriving aircraft check in with the lowest fuel state, in pounds for jets and hours and minutes for helicopters, and with souls on board; they report “see you” when they see the ship and switch to PriFly at 5 miles in visual weather. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-66–11-67')}</a> In marshal, helicopters are separated by 500 ft and jets by 1,000 ft. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-68')}</a> Tiltrotor crews may fly either helicopter or fixed-wing procedures and prefer to stay in airplane mode as long as possible for fuel, survivability and comfort. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-66')}</a></p>
 <p>Departing helicopters leave Case I at or below 300 ft as PriFly directs. In Case III they launch at least a minute apart, climb straight ahead to 500 ft, arc at 3 miles to their departure radials and then climb; with jets on deck too, 2 minutes separate the last helicopter from the first jet. Departing jets turn 10° left after takeoff onto the 350° relative radial. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-62–11-64')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="helo">Helicopters aboard ship</h2>
 <p>Helicopter pilots of every service fly to the deck under the same rules. In visual weather the flight holds in a <Term>Delta</Term> pattern near the ship, plans its descent to meet the recovery time, and lands from the <Term id="Charlie pattern">Charlie pattern</Term>, Figure 2's second pattern. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-15')}</a> At night the helicopter Case I pattern is a left-hand pattern on the port side, extended downwind so the turn to final is finished before the descent, with a straight-in final on the visual landing aids. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-81')}</a> A carrier normally takes its helicopters after its jets. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-42')}</a></p>
 <p>The <Term id="Landing Signal Enlisted (LSE)">LSE</Term> brings the helicopter to its spot with hand signals, sees that it is started, launched, recovered and shut down safely, and that every tie-down is off before liftoff and on after landing. Only the LSE's wave-off and hold signals are orders; the rest are advice. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('2-4')}</a> A helicopter waves off on the LSE's or PriFly's call, on losing radio contact or sight of the LSE, or whenever the pilot judges the approach unsafe, then calls “waving off” parallel to the BRC and re-enters the pattern. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-19')}</a> Ships are not normally turned during a recovery, because the turn can take the helicopter outside its wind and roll limits. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('5-16')}</a> The Army manual prefers 10 kt of relative wind or less from any direction for taking off and landing. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('D-12')}</a> How a rotor behaves in a hover and close to obstacles is covered in <a href={rotor}>Rotor-Wing Principles of Flight</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="dlq">Army deck landing qualification</h2>
 <p>Army pilots who land on ships must be deck landing qualified and current, or be under instruction. In the Army's 1997 manual the rules came from a 1988 agreement between the Army, the Air Force and the Navy, and the manual tells units to confirm them before training. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('1-1, 1-9')}</a> Each qualification starts with ground school and with <Term id="Field deck landing practice (FDLP)">field deck landing practice</Term> ashore, to a spot painted with ship markings, before the landings at sea. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('1-11, 1-14')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Army deck landing qualification and currency" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Army deck landing qualification in FM 1-564 (1997) <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('1-11–1-13')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Deck</th><th scope="col">To qualify, within 10 consecutive days</th><th scope="col">To stay current</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Single- or dual-spot ship</th><td>6 field deck landings, then 6 shipboard landings</td><td>4 shipboard landings in 90 days. Lapsed but 4 in the last 180 days: 4 field and 6 shipboard landings with a current DLQ pilot</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Multispot ship (LPH, LHA, CV), day</th><td>5 field deck landings, then 5 shipboard landings</td><td>4 shipboard landings in the preceding 9 months</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Multispot ship, night</th><td>Day qualified and current, then 6 night field deck landings and 6 night shipboard landings</td><td>6 night shipboard landings in 90 days; after more than 72 hours without one, a day shipboard landing within 24 hours before the next night landing</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Qualification on a single-spot ship counts for multispot ships, but not the other way round, and qualified pilots are considered qualified on carriers too, though routine Army training is not done on them. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('1-13')}</a> Aircrew also had to pass underwater egress training every three years. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('1-14')}</a> Some practical traps: Army tie-down chains did not fit Navy deck fittings, so units had to ask for Navy chains, and night flying with night vision goggles needed the ship's deck lighting turned down. <a href="#source-fm1564">{fm('1-13, 2-4')}</a> The training continues: in March 2024 six UH-60M Black Hawk crews of the 2nd Combat Aviation Brigade made single-spot deck landings on the prepositioning ship USNS Dahl off South Korea to qualify or reset their currency. <a href="#source-dahl">[9]</a></p>
