 <h2 id="stovl">STOVL: the AV-8B aboard ship</h2>
 <p>The <Term record="av-8b">AV-8B Harrier II</Term> gave the Marine Corps a jet that needs neither catapult nor wire: a <Term id="VSTOL">VSTOL</Term> aircraft, able to take off and land vertically or in a short distance, so it can work from amphibious ships and small sites ashore close to the troops it supports. <a href="#source-fas-av8">[7]</a> <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-8')}</a> Its engine's four nozzles swivel from straight back to straight down. It can take off vertically only below its maximum weight, so for a real load of fuel and weapons it makes a short takeoff instead, rotating the nozzles partly down at about 65 kt so that wing lift adds to jet lift; that is why it is usually flown as a short-takeoff, vertical-landing (STOVL) jet. <a href="#source-wiki-harrier">[8]</a> The FAS figures show the difference: about 3,000 kg of useful load for a vertical takeoff against more than 7,700 kg for a short one. <a href="#source-fas-av8">[7]</a> In the hover and at very low speed the wind must enter the intake from straight ahead, or the nose is pushed sideways and the aircraft can roll out of control, and the transitions to and from the hover take great skill, especially in a crosswind. <a href="#source-wiki-harrier">[8]</a> <Term id="Jetborne flight">Jetborne flight</Term> is the controllers' term for that slow flight on engine thrust alone. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-5')}</a></p>
 <p>The Navy's amphibious procedures for fixed-wing aircraft are headed “AV-8 approach procedures”. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-76')}</a> In Case I the jet arrives at an initial point 3 miles astern at 800 ft, wings level and parallel to the BRC, and breaks no more than 5 miles ahead of the ship. On a night aided recovery it flies up the starboard side to a level break at 800 ft, is abeam at 1.0–1.3 DME, passes the 90 at 650 ft, rolls into the groove at 1.3–1.5 DME and 500–550 ft and follows a 3° glideslope to abeam the landing spot, decelerating along the port side one plane-width from the deck edge. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-83–11-84')}</a> In Case III it leaves marshal at 250 kt and 4,000–6,000 ft/min, and arrives at the 12-mile gate at 1,200 ft, where it configures to land; the controllers keep positive control until the LSO takes over. <a href="#source-atc">{atc('11-76, 11-85')}</a> The Harrier's own landing is operationally a near-vertical one with some forward speed. <a href="#source-wiki-harrier">[8]</a> The F-35B, the STOVL version of the F-35, has since replaced the AV-8B in Marine Corps service. <a href="#source-wiki-harrier">[8]</a></p>

