 <p id="source-fas-av8"><strong>[7] Federation of American Scientists, Military Analysis Network, “AV-8B Harrier”.</strong> <a href="https://man.fas.org/dod-101/sys/ac/av-8.htm">Read the page</a>, 27 October 2026: role, basing and useful load.</p>
 <p id="source-wiki-harrier"><strong>[8] Wikipedia, “Harrier jump jet”.</strong> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harrier_jump_jet">Read the article</a>, 27 October 2026. Secondary source, cited for short and vertical takeoff, intake momentum drag, the operational landing and the F-35B.</p>
 <p id="source-dahl"><strong>[9] Grady T. Fontana, “U.S. Army Assault Helicopters Conduct Deck Landings on USNS Dahl”, Seapower, 12 March 2024.</strong> <a href="https://seapowermagazine.org/u-s-army-assault-helicopters-conduct-deck-landings-on-usns-dahl/">Read the article</a>, 27 October 2026; a Military Sealift Command news release.</p>
 <p id="source-wiki-nimitz"><strong>[10] Wikipedia, “Nimitz-class aircraft carrier”.</strong> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nimitz-class_aircraft_carrier">Read the article</a>, 27 October 2026: length 1,092 ft and beam 252 ft overall, flight deck angled at nine degrees, four steam catapults, four arresting wires (three on the last two ships) and four elevators. A secondary source, used for the deck plan only.</p>
<p id="source-navalgazing"><strong>[11] Naval Gazing, “The Nimitz Class”.</strong> <a href="https://www.navalgazing.net/The-Nimitz-Class">Read the page</a>, 27 October 2026: its overhead photograph of the deck, which Figure 1 is traced from, and its account of the four catapults, four wires and four elevators. A secondary source.</p>
 <p>Page-specific excerpts are recorded in the repository review evidence: <a href="https://github.com/burnzoire/oversweep/blob/main/docs/reviews/ship-operations-evidence.md">ship operations evidence</a>.</p>
 <p>Figures 1 to 4 are original drawings, not reproductions of any publication's figures.</p>
 <p class="note">Applicability: written for military pilots of every service. Carrier procedures and numbers are the <Term record="t-45">T-45</Term>'s from a 2001 Navy carrier qualification instruction [1]; ship and air traffic procedures come from a Navy controller training manual [2] and the Army procedures from a 1997 field manual [4], so details will have changed. Air Force crews who land on Navy ships fall under the same Army–Air Force–Navy agreement as the Army [4]; no Air Force manual was read for this revision. The ship's air operations manual, the CV and LHA/LHD NATOPS manuals, your aircraft's flight manual and your service's rules govern. The <a href={caseIII}>Case III Recoveries</a> module covers the instrument recovery; the <a href={airfield}>airfield module</a> covers the shore-based Navy optical landing system and field carrier landing practice.</p>
